# Line endings are DECLARED here rather than left to each clone's core.autocrlf.
#
# WHY (measured 2026-08-19). A script was committed with CRLF into a repo that
# stores LF, so all 124 of its lines read as changed and the real edit was invisible in the diff.
# core.autocrlf was true and should have normalised it on commit. It did not, because the same edit
# had left ONE stray CR in the file, and a lone CR trips git's binary heuristic — at which point EOL
# conversion silently stops for that file. Reproduced from scratch: a CRLF file normalises to LF on
# commit; the same file with one extra CR keeps its CRLF.
#
# That failure is silent, depends on a per-machine setting, and turns a one-line change into a
# whole-file rewrite in the diff — which is also how it hides whatever else the edit did wrong.
#
# THE IMPORTANT PART, because the obvious fix does not work. `* text=auto` does NOT prevent it:
# "auto" means "decide with the heuristic", and the heuristic is exactly what the stray CR defeats.
# Measured, same repro:
#
#     * text=auto eol=crlf   ->  blob keeps CRLF   (bug NOT fixed)
#     * text      eol=crlf   ->  blob normalised   (bug fixed)
#     *.ps1 text  eol=crlf   ->  blob normalised   (bug fixed, for that extension)
#
# So the known text types below are declared `text` WITHOUT `auto`, which skips the heuristic and
# converts unconditionally. A blanket `* text` would fix everything and is deliberately NOT used: it
# would force text handling onto any binary whose type is not declared below, corrupting it on
# checkout. `* text=auto` therefore stays as the safe default for unknown types, and the explicit
# list is where the actual guarantee lives. Adding a new text file type here is what extends it.
#
# WINDOWS-ONLY REPO: a VS 2026 extension, CI is windows-latest with every step in pwsh. So the
# working tree is CRLF everywhere and `eol=crlf` states what is already true rather than imposing a
# convention. LF in the repository, CRLF on disk, decided here rather than per machine.
#
# COST: none at the time of writing. All 290 tracked text blobs were already LF-only, so there is no
# renormalisation commit and `git add --renormalize .` staged nothing. Re-check that rather than
# assuming it if this file is ever changed.

# Safe default for anything not named below: heuristic decides, and a misdetected binary is left
# alone rather than mangled.
* text=auto eol=crlf

# Known text types: no heuristic, always converted. This is the guard.
*.cs            text eol=crlf
*.csproj        text eol=crlf
*.props         text eol=crlf
*.targets       text eol=crlf
*.slnx          text eol=crlf
*.sln           text eol=crlf
*.xaml          text eol=crlf
*.xml           text eol=crlf
*.config        text eol=crlf
*.resx          text eol=crlf
*.json          text eol=crlf
*.yml           text eol=crlf
*.yaml          text eol=crlf
*.md            text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.psm1          text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.vsct          text eol=crlf
*.vsixmanifest  text eol=crlf
*.pkgdef        text eol=crlf
.gitattributes  text eol=crlf
.gitignore      text eol=crlf
.editorconfig   text eol=crlf

# The one LF exception, forward-looking: a shell script with CRLF fails under WSL or a Linux
# container with a "bad interpreter" error that names the wrong problem. None is tracked today.
*.sh            text eol=lf

# Binaries. LOAD-BEARING rather than documentation, though not for the reason first assumed. Git's
# heuristic does catch most binaries on its own — the tracked .vsix is classified correctly with no
# rule at all — so these earn their place on the ones it MISSES: a binary whose early bytes happen to
# look like text gets converted on commit and corrupted. Measured both ways: a file named *.png
# holding plain ASCII with CRLF keeps its bytes with the rule below, and is silently rewritten to LF
# without it. Declare a binary type here before committing one.
*.vsix          binary
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.snk           binary
*.dll           binary
*.exe           binary
*.pdb           binary
*.zip           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.ttf           binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
